The original does a linear scan over the whole message table on every call. This version builds a 64K-entry index table (128 KB) once, on first use, and then each lookup is a single array access.

- **First-match behaviour is kept.** The table is filled from the last entry back to the first, so with duplicate numbers the lowest index wins, exactly as in the original loop.
- **Initialization is thread-safe.** The table is a function-local static, so C++11 and later initialize it exactly once.
- **The cache does not depend on mutable state.** It is derived only from the constant `mesgs` array.
- **Sentinel and fallback.** An entry of `0xFFFF` means "not found". If `MESGS` were ever 65535 or more, so that indices could collide with the sentinel, the function falls back to the original linear scan.

```cpp
const Profile::MESG* Profile::GetMesg(const FIT_UINT16 num)
{
    struct LookupTable
    {
        FIT_UINT16 idx[65536];
        LookupTable()
        {
            for (int i = 0; i < 65536; i++)
                idx[i] = 0xFFFF;
            // Fill backwards so that the first matching entry wins.
            for (int i = MESGS - 1; i >= 0; i--)
                idx[mesgs[i].num] = (FIT_UINT16)i;
        }
    };

    if (MESGS < 0xFFFF)
    {
        static const LookupTable table;
        const FIT_UINT16 i = table.idx[num];
        if (i == 0xFFFF)
            return NULL;
        return &mesgs[i];
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < MESGS; i++)
    {
        if (mesgs[i].num == num)
            return &mesgs[i];
    }

    return NULL;
}
```